summary:
Determines the density of integral binary n-ic forms, ordered by the maximum of their coefficients, with squarefree discriminant (Theorem 1), and with R_f maximal (Theorem 2). The latter is ζ(2)^{−1}ζ(3)^{−1} for n ≥ 3, the arithmetic Bertini theorem conjectured by Poonen for P¹_Z. Both come with power savings (Theorem 6). Consequences:
- ≫ X^{1/2+1/(n−1)} S_n-fields of degree n (Theorem 3);
- Nakagawa's retracted lower bounds for unramified A_n-extensions of quadratic fields (Theorem 4).
The key is the tail estimate (Theorem 5).
- Strong divisibility: the geometric sieve.
- Weak divisibility, odd n: as in Part I, via σ_m into the pairs of symmetric matrices 2 ⊗ Sym²(n), with the Q-invariant (a hyperdeterminant). Distinguished points are counted in the main body (large sieve), the shallow cusp and the deep cusp.
- Weak divisibility, even n: forms are not determinants, so f ↦ xf embeds into singular pencils. A new q-invariant, and a skew-box version of Eskin–Katznelson's count of singular symmetric matrices (§5), complete the count.
The atlas plans Hilbert irreducibility (IG.2) and Siegel sets (AA.3); Mathlib has NumberField.discr. Everything else is routed as sources of ArithmeticStatistics ST.0–ST.3, with GN.4 (lattice-point counts), GN.3, SV.2 (large sieve) and IG.2. Eleven source issues are recorded:
- the n = 4 density in Theorem 1 is wrong (≈ 20.83%, not 21.18%), from a misquoted ν_1;
- Proposition 3.6 is false, and Lemma 6.25 has a degree slip; together they break the deep-cusp bound for even n as written, but an easy repair keeps η_n and Theorems 1–4 and 6;
- Theorem 6.6's exponent fails for n = 4;
- Lemma 7.2 has a sign gap, with an explicit counterexample;
- the odd-m and n = 2 cases and several other steps have gaps, and there are misprints.
The densities α_n(p) and β_n(p), the constants, Q² | Δ, (19)–(20) and the Appendix congruence were checked by computation.

routes.reason:
ST.0 owns families, heights, local conditions and local densities. For binary forms that covers the box height, the discriminant and Stickelberger's congruence, strong and weak divisibility, and the densities ν_0, ν_1, α_n(p) and β_n(p) (Appendix, corrected in E1). ST.1 owns integral invariant theory and orbit parametrizations, with stabilizers and degenerate loci. That covers:
- the ring R_f of a binary form;
- the SL_n-representation 2 ⊗ Sym²(n) with its invariant form, J_f[2]-stabilizers, distinguished orbits and the orbit criterion for even n;
- the prehomogeneous space 2 ⊗ g ⊗ (g + 1), whose hyperdeterminant is the Q-invariant, with Q² | Δ and the corrected Proposition 3.6;
- the embeddings σ_m, with |Q| = m for odd n;
- the singular embedding f ↦ xf with the new q-invariant for even n.
The monic analogues are routed to the same stages by PAPER-BHARGAVA-SHANKAR-WANG-22. None of these statements is in the atlas or the libraries.

ST.2 owns uniform arithmetic-family orbit counts in fundamental domains, with cusp and reducible contributions, and the uniform tail estimates needed for infinitely many local conditions. As narrowed by RS-07, it consumes generic lattice-point estimates from GN.4. The paper's analytic core is exactly this:
- the tail estimates of Theorem 5;
- for odd n, main body by the large sieve, shallow cusp, and deep cusp by the Q-invariant (§4);
- for even n, fibering over the row space of the singular B, then main body, shallow cusp with the q-invariant, and deep cusp (§6);
- Corollary 6.27 and the sieve Theorem 7.1.
None of these statements is in the atlas or the libraries.

ST.3 owns field-counting asymptotics, with degree, signature, Galois-group and local-condition restrictions. The paper contributes:
- the densities of squarefree discriminants and of maximal orders for binary forms (Theorems 1, 2 and 6);
- the arithmetic Bertini theorem for P¹_Z;
- the lower bound ≫ X^{1/2+1/(n−1)} for S_n-fields (Theorem 3), via quasi-reduction (Lemma 7.2, corrected);
- the count of unramified A_n-extensions of quadratic fields (Theorem 4).
None of these statements is in the atlas or the libraries.

sourceIssues.correction:
Here W = W_{n+1}, so f_{A,B} is an (n + 1)-ic form and Δ(A, B) is homogeneous of degree 2n(n + 1). Then |Δ(A′, B′)| = X^{−2n}|Δ(A, B)| > X^{−2−κ} only, so the printed conclusion does not follow. Repair:
- discard, besides V^{Δ small}, the forms with |f(0, 1)| < H(f)^{1−κ/(2n−2)} (O(X^{n+1−κ/(2n−2)}) of them), and work in dyadic height ranges;
- then for (A, B) ∈ L(1), Δ(A, B) = Δ(xf) = Δ(f)f(0, 1)² ≫ X^{2n−κ″} with κ″ = κn/(n − 1);
- with the corrected Proposition 3.6 (E2), det(B_0^top(B_0^top)^t) ≫ Y^{2(g+1)}X^{−2κ″};
- so |q| ≪ Y^{(g+1)²}X^{κ″}∏_{i≤g+1}L_i in (66), and Theorem 6.24 holds with X^{n+1+κ″} in place of X^{n+1+κ/2}.
In §6.5 (κ = (2n − 2)/(88n⁶)) this changes ξ_n in Theorem 5(c) and Corollary 6.27 from 1/(88n⁵) to 1/(44n⁵). The saving η_n = 1/(88n⁶) and Theorems 1–4 and 6 are unchanged.

Part I (for monic polynomials) gives the divisibility but not the coprimality (m, b_0) = 1, which the choice of r needs. Repair, for each odd p | m:
- move the double root to (1 : 0), so p | a_0, a_1 and p ∤ a_2, and p² | a_0 by the Appendix congruence;
- apply (x, y) ↦ (x, pt·x + y), which changes a_0/p² mod p by (a_1/p)t + a_2t² with a_2 ≢ 0;
- this quadratic has at most two roots mod p ≥ 3, so some t gives p³ ∤ a_0;
- combine the primes by CRT.

